andreas Laing is on the way to his  current workplace today I'll be  selecting video sequences from my last  trip to Cameroon and preparing them for  processing and editing to be used later  in the video installation at the  exhibition the photographer and video  artists current studio is under the roof  of the German Foreign Office it  commissioned long to travel to Cameroon  and follow the trail of Germany's  colonial history as well as that of his  own family his great-grandfather was a  soldier in the German colonial  protection force his research unearthed  photos finding them was really a stroke  of luck there was his name in his rank  written underneath I've never really  known what rank he had it said sergeant  couplet on our team hippopotamus lungs  photos depict remnants of the colonial  era they're snapshots such as this  cabinet makers workshop with colonial  style furniture spawned off it was  already there and we just had to  encounter each other with the photo and  I the colonial era is still present for  the people there as well  such as for this man from the village of  banana the name was given to the  settlement by the Germans  I know that's it see that explains that  on the one hand there were certainly  even friendships but on the other hand  that there were beatings there was often  violence and despotism in the Foreign  Office archives lung found a contract in  which Germany and France agreed on new  borders for Cameroon and Congo that was  an imperialist action the population had  to just cope with it or not or there  were rebellions for Andrea's lung this  part of Germany's history has not yet  been processed he believes his work will  spark uncomfortable but necessary  questions about the country's colonial  past
 

